or pulling out features because  it's just not gonna work okay and that's  actually one of the reasons that in the  early Facebook face recognition  algorithms what they would do is they  would actually the first thing they  would do is they would pick out the  corners of the eyes the corners of the  nose and the corners of the mouth and  they would map it onto a standard  stencil so that everyone's eyes nose and  mouth were at the same kind of geometry  so even if even if one person had maybe  wider eyes and a taller face or narrow  our eyes and a shorter face they would  get mapped on to this standard template  so that the pixels in the mouth region  the nose region and the eye region could  be compared apples to apples from image  to image and that gives you a much much  better classification performance and  dimensionality reduction feature  extraction just by cropping and aligning  your data okay so we as humans have this  innate ability to recognize  translational invariance in images okay  so if I see an image of a truck  over here or a truck over here doesn't  make much of a difference to me it makes  a huge difference to the SVD and to  image classification so you have to  really crop and align based on the  object you're you're looking for okay  other examples of invariance is that the  SVD cannot handle very well that we can  handle our rotations so in fact humans  have a really good sense of what I'm  going to call camera symmetries so if  you look at an object from any different  perspective you can you know very easily  tell what that object is and classify it  regardless of translations rotations and  scale and variances again not so for the  singular value decomposition this is not  built to have those invariances of  translation rotation and scale modern  methods in optimization are starting to  get dimensionality reduction that has  and variances and in fact that's why  people like convolutional neural network  so much is because they capture this  translational invariance that is so easy  for us to handle an images so another  example I like to show and I'm gonna  code this up in MATLAB and Python is  this idea that if I have a data matrix X  that looks like this where I'm gonna  shade it in that means that the value is  1 and the clear region means that a  value is 0 so this is my data matrix X  this is a Rank 1 matrix there is one  object there's one linearly independent  row and column that describes this  entire data matrix ok maybe 3 - I think  it's yeah it's low rank a low rank data  matrix but if I start to rotate that  square then and compute the SVD my rank  is going to increase and increase and  increase and increase until when I have  a diamond like this again it's the same  object to us humans we recognize that  this is the same low rank object that  this is except now when I compute the  SVD because it's doing row wise and  column wise correlations this has very  high rank maybe you know in the hundreds  depending on the resolution of the image  just might have a rank of a hundred even  though we recognize that it's still kind  of a 1 Rank 1 object it's like one  coherent square we just rotated it so  that's a cautionary tale that you have  to be very careful when you're using the  SVD for systems with translations  rotations scale invariance especially if  you're using this to build dynamical  systems models like gelec and projection  models if this is a physical system  evolving in time if you take the SVD of  a travelling wave as it goes from left  to right you're not going to get one  coherent wave with a wave speed you're  gonna get some weird linear combination  of this wave at lots and lots of  positions along its track and it's not  going to make physical sense so I want  to point this out you know if this  matrix X has the meaning  X has spacial so let's call this space  and time dimensions so it's some spatial  measurement evolving in time then the  SVD is essentially going to give you a  space time separation of variables just  like what we do in solving partial  differential equations just like what we  do with the Fourier transform and so if  you have a system that has traveling  waves or rotations or scale in variances  this isn't going to work very well okay  big caution big caveat and it's actually  a pretty exciting area of ongoing  research is how to take these these very  simple linear algebra techniques and  adapt them so that they can handle  rotations and translations and things  like that  okay so we'll code this up and see how  it works and kind of see these pitfalls  in code next thank you

